<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asilah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arzila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asilah, historically Arzila, is a fortified town on the Atlantic coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>. Portugal captured it in 1471 under Afonso V and held it until 1550, and King Sebastian landed there in 1578 before his defeat at Alcácer Quibir. Its Portuguese ramparts still enclose the old medina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asilah.jpg" alt="Asilah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Prazeres · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asilah_seafront.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Asilah is a fortified town on the north-western tip of the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, about 31 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>. Its ramparts and gateworks remain fully intact. The history of the area reaches back to about 1500 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> occupied a site called Silis, Zili, Zilis or Zilil, which is being excavated at Dchar Jdid, some 12 kilometres north-east of the present town. That site was once taken for the Roman stronghold Ad Mercuri but is now accepted to be Zilil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town itself was first built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>, and the Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">Al-Hakam II</a> rebuilt it in 966. Its walls were first built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> and restored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> and Wattasids. The Portuguese conquered it in 1471, rebuilt the walls to resist artillery and shrank the perimeter for easier control. In 1578 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-of-portugal">Sebastian of Portugal</a> used Asilah as a base for his troops in a planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> that ended in his death, which led to the Portuguese succession crisis of 1580. The Saadians briefly regained the town in 1589, and then lost it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a> took it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1692 and built the Grand Mosque in the former kasbah soon afterwards. In the 19th and 20th centuries it served as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and in 1829 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> bombarded it as a punishment for Moroccan piracy. Moulay Ahmed er-Raisuni, a local pirate who rose to power and declared himself pasha of the region, built his palace there in 1909 and gained wealth partly through kidnappings and ransoms. From 1912 to 1956 Asilah was part of Spanish Morocco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old walled medina dates mostly from the Portuguese occupation of the 15th and 16th centuries and afterwards. It has two main gates, Bab Homar and Bab al-Qasaba, and a rectangular Portuguese tower called Borj al-Hamra. Its buildings are painted white and decorated with murals made during the International Cultural Moussem of Asilah, an art festival started in 1978 by the mayor Mohamed Benaissa and the painter Mohamed Melehi, which helped renew the town. Asilah is a seaside resort and a place of weekend visits from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a>. Its Kahal Synagogue, built in 1824, was restored and reopened in 2022.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asilah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Afonso V added Ksar es-Seghir in 1458 and <b>Arzila</b> and Tangier in 1471.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a></li><li><blockquote>After the last Marinid sultan was killed in Fez in 1465, a sharif ruled the city until Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi, who had just lost <b>Arzila</b> to Portugal in 1471, captured Fez in 1472 and proclaimed himself sultan.</blockquote><a href="/state/wattasid-sultanate">Wattasid Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
